## Lichen planus and lichenoid eruptions

## Urticaria

- **Definition** - presence of wheals or angioedema that is localised dermal oedema secondary to a temporary increase in capillary permeability
- **DDx of urticaria:** 
![](img/ddx_of_urticaria.png)
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - **HPI** - site, onset, duration, quality, and associated exposures:
    - <u>Site</u> - characterise site of eruption, as bradykinergic angioedema has a tendancy of developing over the face, upper extremities or around genitalia
    - <u>Onset</u> - urticaria present for \< 6 weeks is considered to be acute, and chronic if \> 6 weeks (CSU is the most common cause of chronic urticaria)
    - <u>Duration of eruption</u>:
      - Individual wheals typically resolve within 24h
      - Angioedema typically takes longer to resolver (24-72h)
    - <u>Quality</u>:
      - Wheals are typically pruritic w/ no pain
      - Angioedema are typically painful, and not pruritic
    - <u>Associated exposures</u> - note exposure within the last 24h:
      - Food
      - Medications
      - Inhalants
      - Cotact (e.g. latex)
  - **Associated Sx:**
    - <u>Swelling of the face</u> - note any swelling of the lips, face, tongue
    - <u>Wheezing or SOB</u> - suggest respiratory comprimise
    - <u>Abdominal pain</u> - suggest GI comprimise
  - **Drug Hx**
  - **FHx** - FHx of angioedema
- **P/E:**
  - <u>Active wheals</u>: 
  ![](img/wheals.png)
- **Ix:**
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, LRFT, ESR, TFT, iron studies
  - **Total IgE and specific IgE** - to possible allergens e.g. shellfish, peanutes, house dust mites
  - **ANA** - +ve for SLE and urticarial vasculitis
  - **C3/4** - consider C1 esterase inhibitor activity if low
  - +/- **skin Bx** - if urticarial vasculitis suspected
  - **+/- challenge test** - to confirm physical urticaria
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Removal of trigger</u> - detailed allergic Hx to identify inciting allergen
    - <u>Pharmacological therapy</u> - non-sedating antihistamines are first line, with mast cell stabilisers, leuktriene receptor antagonists, or even systemic therapy

## Bullous disorders

- **Definition** - blistering at any level of the skin, reflected by <u>dysfunction of cutaneous cell adhesions</u>

### Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis

- **Definition** - drug-induced medical emergency a/w extensive mucocutaneous blistering in association w/ a high mortality rate
- **Etiology of TEN** - often drug-induced:
  - Anticonvulsants
  - Sulphonamides
  - Sulphonylureas
  - NSAIDs
  - Allopurinol (HLA-B5801)
  - Antiretroviral therapy
- **Clinical features of TEN** - usually 1-4 weeks after drug commencement w/ Hx of culprit drug:
  - **Systemic upset** - often pyrexic with other non-specific S/S
  - **Acute onset of skin manifestations** - progress epidermal blistering and epidermal loss with involvement of underlying skin, initially **on the trunk but rapidly involving all skin**:
    - <u>Epidermal blistering</u> - erythema and blister formation w/ sheets of blisters coalesce and denude, exposing the underlying skin
    - <u>Exposure of underlying skiin</u> - underlying skin becomes erythematous, oedematous, and is often very painful
  - **Mucous membrane involvement** - blistering of mucous membrane is usual, often w/ urethral or occular involvement (scarring may result in problems in survivors)
- **DDx of TEN:**
  - <u>Staphylococcus scalded skin syndrome</u> (SSSS) - differentiated in an adult patient with a culprit drug
- **Assessment of disease severity** - SCORTEN: 
![](img/scoreten_for_ten.png)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx** - Mx in a dedicated dermatology ward, ICU, or burn unit:
    - <u>Identification and discontinuation of causative drug</u> - most important factors
    - <u>Supportive care</u> - regular sterile dressing and emolliments, fluid management, monitor for urethral or occular involvement, and monitor for infections
    - <u>No role of immunomodulation</u> - systemic corticosteroid are C/I as a/w increased mortality, while no good evidence for IVIG

    
    ![](img/evidence_of_ivig_in_ten.png)

### Immunobullous disease

- **Definition** - heterogenous group of blistering disorders w/ an immunological basis
- **Common immunobullous diseases** - differentiated by clinical features, serology and age of onset:
  - Bullous pemphigoid
  - Pemphigus
  - Dermatitis herpetiformis
  - Linear IgA disease
  - Epidermolysis bullous
  - Bullous lupus erythromatosus

  ![](img/age_of_onset_of_immunobullous_disorders.png) 
  ![](img/clinical_features_and_ix_findings_of_immunobullous_disorders.png)

1.  Bullous pemphigoid

    - **Definition** - most common immunobullous disease worldwide causing tense bulla and is an autoimmune disease of the elderly
    - **Epidemiology:**
      - <u>Prevalence</u> - most common immunobullous disease
      - <u>Demographic</u>:
        - Age - disease of the elderly (average age of onset \> 65y)
        - Sex - M=F
        - Comorbidities - a/w neurological disorders (e.g. stroke, PD, dementia)
    - **Pathophysiology of BP:**
      - <u>AutoAb production</u> - detectable autoAb (e.g. BP-230, BP-180) production directed against hemi-desmisomal Ags (e.g. BPAg-1 \[intracellular\], BPAg-2 \[transmembrane type XVII collagen\])
      - <u>Complement activation and inflammation</u> - resulting in local inflammation, hemi-desmosomal damage, loss of cell-cell junction and subsequently subepidermal blistering
    - **Clinical features of BP:**
      - <u>Prodromal cutaneous manifestation</u> - lengthy prodromal itchy, urticated, erythematous rash prior to the onset of tense bullae, often w/ milia formation (tense milk spots) due to BM disruption
      - <u>Tense bullae formation</u> - fluid filled vesicles, characterstically over the **trunk, flexures, and limbs** 
      ![](img/large_tense_bullae_in_bp.png)
      - <u>Mucosal blistering</u> - most commonly over the oral mucosa
    - **Ix:**
      - **Skin Bx** - split in two and one sent for direct immunofluoresence and one for FFPE and histology:
        - <u>Histology</u> - subepidermal blistering w/ eosinophilia rich infiltrate
        - <u>Direct IMF</u> - IgG and C3 staining at the basememnt membrane

        
        ![](img/direct_imf_of_bp.png)
      - **Serology** - indirect IF and ELISA for BP-230 and BP-180:
        - <u>Indirect IF of serum</u> - IgG for w/ +ve titres of circulating anti-epidermal Ab (70%)
        - <u>ELISA</u> - +ve for BP-180 and BP-230
      - **Microbiology** - blister fluids for culture to exclude viral or bacterial infection
    - **Mx:**
      - **Principles of Mx:**
        - <u>Escalation of immunosuppressive therapy</u>:
          - Topical potent corticosteroids
          - Systemic corticosteroid
          - Biologics
          - IVIG

        
        ![](img/evidence_for_tx_of_bp.png)

2.  Pemphigus

3.  Dermatitis hermetiformis

4.  Linear IgA disease

5.  Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita

6.  Porphyria cutanea tarda and pseudoporphyria

